# LF everywhere, in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# This is not tidiness. verify-links.py asserts that every text file in the
# artifact is LF-only, and the artifact is built from OldVersion/, which is
# LF-only on disk. Git's default Windows behaviour (core.autocrlf=true) stores
# LF but checks out CRLF, so a Windows clone would produce a CRLF artifact and
# fail that check locally while CI, on Linux, passed it. Pinning eol here makes
# the two agree. Binary files are detected and left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf
